<!DOCTYPE html><html xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
<head>
<title>Dilata: Dilation of pixels of a selected value in raster files</title>
<meta content="text/html; charset=ISO-8859-1" http-equiv="Content-Type"/></head>

<body>
  <!--Aquí inicia la part corresponent al titol de la MSA. Aquesta part s'ha d'editar desde el document WPS (aplicacio.mps).-->
<h1><span id="wpstitol"/></h1>
<!--Aquí acaba la part corresponent titol de la MSA editable de el WPS.-->

<!--Aquí inicia la part corresponent a les opcions de de la MSA. Aquesta part s'ha d'editar desde el document WPS (aplicacio.mps).-->
<span id="wpsopcions"/>
<!--Aquí acaba la part corresponent a les opcions de de la MSA editable de el WPS.-->
  <p>The syntax section of this document specifies the different directions that the dilation process can be. Rasters subjected to this module are typically masks, binary or not, where we want to dilate all values other than the background value (NoData), or thematic maps with different values in the legend previously rasterized (e.g. lines) that we want to dilate (in these cases, the program will dilate these structures, respecting the rest of the map).</p>
  <p>The dilation can also be used as an approximation to a directional or radial buffer with a pixel size (or pixel by square root of 2 for expansion on all neighbours), to apply around raster areas or rasterized linear elements. The richness of expansion types that this module supports makes it complementary to the TiraVec and BufDist modules.</p>
  <p>The process, which supports both files with integer and real values, also acts on the image borders.</p>
  <p>More information: <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dilation_(morphology)">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dilation_(morphology)</a></p>
<hr/>
